          "question": "My husband's face is drooping on one side and he can't speak properly. Is this a stroke?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now. Do not wait to see if it gets better.**\n\nI can't tell from here whether this is a stroke. But your husband's symptoms match the warning signs that the emergency guidance says must be treated as a stroke until proven otherwise. Treatment works best when it starts quickly. (Source: *Sudden neurological symptoms that may indicate a stroke*.)\n\nWhile you wait for help:\n\n- **Note the time** the symptoms started, or the last time he was known to be well. Emergency staff will ask for this.\n- **Check the other signs** from the same guidance: weakness or numbness in one arm (ask him to raise both arms and see if one drifts down), sudden numbness or weakness in a leg or one side of the body, sudden confusion, sudden vision loss, sudden loss of balance, or a sudden severe headache.\n- **Do not give him food, drink, or medicine** unless the emergency team tells you to.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
